Optimise the systems carefully.

Do not simulate every distant ship, pirate fleet, battle or sea monster with full physics at all times.

Use efficient world streaming and only perform detailed simulations when necessary.

Maintain good performance without unnecessarily removing visual detail.

# 20. FINAL REQUIREMENTS

The goal is to make Oceanbound feel like a living olden-times sailing world.

The player should be able to peacefully explore and trade, become a notorious pirate, rescue innocent ships, join pirate conflicts, defeat powerful faction leaders or build an enormous trading vessel.

Pirates must attack innocent NPC ships independently. Different factions must have their own relationships, fleets, captains and leaders. Factions should fight, form alliances and change their relationships throughout the player's journey.

Some pirate groups should travel with enormous capital ships, supported by smaller escort vessels.

Pirate bosses should feel like major encounters with powerful ships, large crews and valuable rewards.

Sea monsters should remain extremely rare and capable of changing encounters.

Merchant ships should have different sizes, inventories and specialities. Fishing trawlers must exclusively sell provisions and fresh water, while rare merchants should offer specialised equipment and materials.

The player must be able to customise their ship's flag, unlock rare designs and collect special dyes.

Crew members must automatically receive provisions and water, with clear warnings whenever supplies are running low or depleted.

The player must have access to a large variety of cannons and hull upgrades, with their own separate management tabs.

Crew assignment should be easy through one-click automatic role buttons, while still allowing manual control.

The player must be able to press Space to gradually stop their ship, with realistic stopping behaviour for different ship sizes.

The player must also receive warnings whenever a crew member drops below 25 health, including the crew member's name and current health.

Keep early-game construction relatively simple using timber, iron, cloth and gold. Introduce specialised materials gradually for more advanced and expensive late-game upgrades.

**The most important progression goal is to start with a tiny wooden boat and eventually build an enormous, heavily armed and heavily reinforced sailing ship with a large, well-organised crew.**

Keep the existing simplified naval combat system. Do not introduce complicated individual cannon controls or direct sword fighting.

Make every feature functional rather than a visual placeholder. NPC ships must interact, pirates must operate independently, factions must fight and form alliances, monsters must behave according to their type, music must respond to gameplay, trading must work, crew assignments must actually affect ship performance, and damage must have lasting consequences.

Only implement these updates while preserving all existing features, controls, graphics and gameplay wherever possible.